\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.1}{\ignorespaces Accuracy is not reliability. Left: a known-class input, confidently misclassified (miscalibration). Right: an input from an unseen class, confidently assigned to a known class with no indication of uncertainty (no OOD flag).}}{3}{figure.caption.6}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.2}{\ignorespaces All four research questions are instances of one overarching question — which design choice governs which reliability aspect — each resolved by a specific, named piece of evidence.}}{6}{figure.caption.12}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces One 5-way, 5-shot episode: a support grid of 5 labelled images per class, and a query block of 15 unlabelled images per class to be classified. A fresh set of 5 classes is sampled for every episode, and the 20 test classes are never used to train the adapter.}}{20}{figure.caption.18}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces The B-PEFT pipeline. Support and query images pass through the frozen backbone with a small trainable adapter inserted at each stage; the resulting features feed a parameter-free prototype head, whose similarity logits are read as either softmax or evidential (Dirichlet) probabilities. Only the adapter (orange) and, in the evidential interpretation, its two evidence-affine parameters are trained; the backbone (grey) is frozen and the prototype head has no learnable weights of its own.}}{24}{figure.caption.19}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces The two adapter architectures compared throughout this thesis: the parallel bottleneck, a down-project/ReLU/up-project side branch added at every stage; and LoRA, a low-rank update inside a single frozen $1{\times }1$ convolution.}}{25}{figure.caption.22}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.3}{\ignorespaces One set of raw similarity logits, read two ways: softmax probabilities scored by MSP/energy, or Dirichlet evidence scored by vacuity. The two interpretations differ by exactly the two evidence-affine parameters.}}{28}{figure.caption.26}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.4}{\ignorespaces The full experimental design: the base grid (120 runs), re-scored with 4 OOD scores and a post-hoc refit at no extra training cost, followed by the matched-budget experiment (48 runs) and the rank sweep (21 runs) — 189 training runs in total.}}{32}{figure.caption.28}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.1}{\ignorespaces Full fine-tuning versus the parallel-bottleneck adapter versus the linear probe: trainable parameters and accuracy, CIFAR-FS 5-shot, ResNet-18.}}{36}{figure.caption.31}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.2}{\ignorespaces Main-effects heatmap: share of variance ($\eta ^2$, \%) each design axis explains for each outcome. Accuracy is dominated by shots; ECE by head; neither OOD pool has one dominant factor.}}{37}{figure.caption.33}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.3}{\ignorespaces OOD AUROC by scoring rule, split by which objective trained the model, for far- and near-OOD pools. The same score gives nearly the same AUROC regardless of which objective produced the logits it scores; the gap between scores dwarfs the gap between objectives.}}{40}{figure.caption.36}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.4}{\ignorespaces The 16 matched pairs: which adapter wins on accuracy and ECE, by backbone. The accuracy winner is constant (bottleneck); the ECE winner flips with the backbone.}}{42}{figure.caption.39}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.5}{\ignorespaces The trainable-parameter-count reversal behind \autoref {tab:param-reversal}: bottleneck is the larger adapter on ResNet-18, LoRA is the larger adapter on MobileNetV3-Small.}}{43}{figure.caption.40}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.6}{\ignorespaces $\Delta $ECE (LoRA $-$ bottleneck), unmatched versus matched budget, by backbone and head. The gap stays intact on ResNet-18 and roughly halves on MobileNetV3-Small — the backbone determines the sign, the budget only modulates the magnitude.}}{44}{figure.caption.43}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.7}{\ignorespaces Residual parameter-count mismatch for each matched-budget pair, all under 3.1\% — the matched arms are close enough in size that the budget confound of \autoref {tab:param-reversal} genuinely no longer applies.}}{44}{figure.caption.44}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.8}{\ignorespaces Pooled ECE before and after the post-hoc refit, against the plain-softmax reference. The refit closes most, but not all, of the gap to softmax.}}{47}{figure.caption.46}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.9}{\ignorespaces Change in OOD AUROC after the post-hoc refit, per pool and cell. Most changes cluster near zero; the minority exception is visible as the scattered points furthest from it.}}{48}{figure.caption.47}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.10}{\ignorespaces Effect on inference latency of backbone, adapter type, and head interpretation, measured on a canonical edge-proxy CPU session.}}{49}{figure.caption.49}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.1}{\ignorespaces Every design factor tested against every reliability outcome: which links were found, which were tested and not supported, and which are partial. Adapter budget alone does not reach calibration or OOD ranking; architecture and scoring rule do the work that a first look at parameter count alone would suggest budget was doing.}}{55}{figure.caption.52}%
